these images show the projection of  power a few of the dozens of offshore  outposts and military bases china has  built in recent years  ownership of these islands reefs and  built up sandbanks in the south china  sea and east china sea is disputed  several states claim the islands for  their rich fishing grounds possible oil  deposits and strategic locations  there have been showdowns vietnam says  this is one of its boats being rammed by  a chinese vessel on the open sea  and filipinos have protested a chinese  law authorizing its coast guard to  destroy other country structures on  islands it claims  just a few reasons why one of china's  indo-pacific neighbors decided it needs  to act  last week australia sealed a deal to  build nuclear-powered submarines with  state-of-the-art u.s and british  technology  and so friends orcas is born  a new enhanced trilateral security  partnership between australia the united  kingdom and the united states  china responded immediately and sternly  australia is solely responsible for the  difficult situation of current chinese  australian relations  australia must correctly recognize the  reasons for setbacks in the two  countries ties and think carefully  whether to treat china as a partner or a  threat  other reactions were mixed  strengthening defense cooperation  between the u.s britain and australia is  important for peace in the indo-pacific  we hope the decision  won't  disturb  the situation in the region  but given the region's disputes and  military buildups  more disturbances seem practically  assured  lots to unpack here let's bring in  garema mohan a fellow at the german  marshall fund of the united states with  a research focus on the indo-pacific  karima welcome let's just talk about  friday's quad leaders meeting first now  the leaders of the united states japan  australia and india are meeting in the  backdrop of last week's orcas security  pact what can we expect from this court  meeting  so while the orcas is primarily a  security pack the quad has a much  broader remit so i think we have to see  the orcas and quad as complementary and  not competitive initiatives from the  meeting on friday we can see  i'm sure we'll certainly see a few  announcements around the three working  groups that were created in the last  meeting around vaccines where the quad  is on track to deliver one billion doses  to the indo-pacific by next year on  critical technologies on climate change  we'll certainly see a new initiative  perhaps around regional connectivity and  infrastructure to offer an alternative  to china's bri  and we will see  an announcement around maritime security  cooperation so a really broad remit for  the quad leaders summit this time but  you also mentioned that there were  complementary initiatives the court  dialogue and the orcas security fact  another thing that is common to them of  course is that they both essentially  counter chinese influence in the  indo-pacific even though the leaders  don't explicitly name china and this is  in addition to existing security packs  such as those between the united states  and japan as i wonder therefore if all  this alliance building against china if  this is a good thing for the  indo-pacific so let me start by saying  that it is china's assertive behavior  particularly vis-a-vis the quad  countries that has created the  conditions for these four partners to  work together in different permutations  and combinations there is really a  networked security architecture emerging  in the indo-pacific but i have to say  that the quad particularly under the  biden administration is not just an  anti-china security alliance first of  all it is not even an alliance but under  the biden administration in its  particular iteration it has become much  broader it argues that it wants to  provide solutions to regional problems  public goods and therefore it is a more  positive thing  as i mentioned the quad leaders summit  this one and also the previous one  focused really on bigger problems like  covet  climate change technology it's also a  recognition of the fact that the  challenge from china is not just in the  security realm it is actually much  broader and it exists extends to trade  technology  political influence infrastructure and  therefore the four countries are coming  together in various coalitions to  provide alternatives and solutions for  that in the indo-pacific that might be  the wider remit potentially with quad in  particular but then i wonder how china  views this given particularly the  statements that have been coming out  from china in which the term  cold war mentality keeps getting  repeated in terms of the quad and the  orcas so how do you view the chinese  response and how do you see this  expanding moving forward  well china's response is of course  expected it is always displeasure one  has to note that china works in  coalitions of its own so it's not just  these four countries that are working in  these coalitions china is also working  in different combinations interestingly  there's also russia-india-china  trilateral so there are opportunities  for dialogue and positive collaboration  if countries are so interested but i  have to bring up the recent example of  the german frigate buy-in which uh  requested a port call with china and  that was rejected now for your a lot of  european countries that are taking a  more inclusive uh view of the  indo-pacific which is more focused on  cooperation and less on competition  china by rejecting this port call sent a  very clear signal  that even inclusivity in this really  mild form is not acceptable  and therefore the chinese behavior  particularly increasing assertiveness  vis-a-vis europe as well does not create  the conditions necessary for  the cooperation that it says that it  seeks  we leave it there for the time being but  thank you so much for joining us today  garema mohan from the german marshall  fund of the 